      "q": "Managers privately choose purchasing effort. Relative to no control, targeted audits prevent $25,000 in losses and cost $12,000 plus $3,000 in delay. Broad audits prevent $31,000 and cost $24,000 plus $10,000 in delay. With targeted audits, an additional verified-savings bonus would prevent another $8,000 in losses but cost $5,000 to pay and verify. Managers can inflate reported savings if verification is dropped. Which policy is best on these stated effects?",
